<section xml:id="sec-mod3-bridge-reading">
  <title>Bridge Reading Guide</title>
  <p>Reading: Bauldry, Section 2.1. Estimated time: 35 minutes.</p>

    <p>
      <alert>Read:</alert> Bauldry &#xa7;2.1 (Introduction to Real Analysis,
      pp. 37<ndash/>52, approximately): <q>The Topology of <m>\R</m>.</q>
    </p>
    <p>
      This reading is your first direct encounter with the material
      you will study in MAT 5610. Bauldry &#xa7;2.1 develops the topology
      of the real line: neighborhoods, open and closed sets, interior
      and boundary points, and limit points (accumulation points). Every
      definition in this section is written in the set and function
      language of Module 3. The goal of this reading is not to master
      topology (that is MAT 5610's job) but to see concretely how the
      vocabulary you have built is deployed in graduate-level analysis.
    </p>
    <p>
      As you read, notice the following:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Neighborhoods are defined using absolute value (from <xref ref="ch-mod2"/>)
            and described as open intervals, which are sets.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <q>Deleted neighborhoods</q> are formed using set difference
            (a Module 3 operation).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Open and closed sets are defined in terms of set-theoretic
            properties (every point is an interior point; the complement
            is open).
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Accumulation points are defined by a quantified statement
            about neighborhoods, and the distinction between finite and
            infinite sets (cardinality) comes up as soon as you study
            them (see <xref ref="ex-mod3-br-3"/>).
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      The guided questions below are not submitted. Write your answers
      in your notebook as you read; they are designed to ensure you
      engage actively with the text rather than reading passively.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       GUIDED QUESTIONS
  ============================================================ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-br-1">
    <title>Neighborhoods as Sets</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Bauldry defines the <term><m>\delta</m>-neighborhood</term> of a point
        <m>c \in \R</m> as the set
        <m>N(c; \delta) = \{x \in \R : |x - c| \lt \delta\}</m>.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            What interval (in standard notation) is <m>N(c;\delta)</m>?
          </li>
          <li>
            Bauldry also defines the <term>deleted <m>\delta</m>-neighborhood</term>
            <m>N^*(c;\delta) = N(c;\delta) \setminus \{c\}</m>.
            Write this set in set-builder notation and as a union of
            two intervals. Why is the point <m>c</m> removed?
          </li>
          <li>
            Is <m>N(c;\delta)</m> an open set, a closed set, or neither?
            (Look up Bauldry's definition of open set and apply it.)
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-br-2">
    <title>Set Operations in Topology</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Bauldry defines the <term>interior</term> of a set <m>S</m> as the
        set of all interior points of <m>S</m>, where <m>c</m> is an
        interior point if some neighborhood of <m>c</m> is contained
        in <m>S</m> (i.e., <m>\exists \delta \gt 0</m> such that
        <m>N(c;\delta) \subseteq S</m>).
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Using Module 3 set notation, what does
            <m>N(c;\delta) \subseteq S</m> mean in terms of membership?
          </li>
          <li>
            Bauldry states that an open set equals its own interior.
            What does this say about the relationship between a set
            and the subsets used to define it?
          </li>
          <li>
            Find one set from Zorn Chapter 1 that is open and one
            that is closed (in the Bauldry sense). Verify your choices
            by checking the definition.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-br-3">
    <title>Accumulation Points and Cardinality</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A point <m>c</m> is an <term>accumulation point</term> (or
        <term>limit point</term>) of a set <m>S</m> if every deleted
        neighborhood of <m>c</m> contains at least one point of <m>S</m>:
        <md>\forall \delta \gt 0,\quad N^*(c;\delta) \cap S \neq \emptyset.</md>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Write this definition out using the set-builder and quantifier
            notation from Modules <xref ref="ch-mod1" text="custom">1</xref> and 3.
          </li>
          <li>
            Is 0 an accumulation point of the set
            <m>\{1/n : n \in \N\}</m>? Explain by checking the
            definition: for any <m>\delta \gt 0</m>, can you find
            <m>n \in \N</m> such that <m>1/n \in N^*(0;\delta)</m>?
          </li>
          <li>
            The set <m>\{1/n : n \in \N\}</m> is countably infinite
            (it is in bijection with <m>\N</m>). Its set of accumulation
            points, however, contains only one point. What is that point?
            Why does the size (cardinality) of the set not determine how
            many accumulation points it has?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-br-4">
    <title>Notation and Vocabulary Alignment</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        As you read Bauldry &#xa7;2.1, you will encounter notation and
        terminology that may differ slightly from Zorn's.
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            Does Bauldry use <m>\subseteq</m> or <m>\subset</m> for
            the subset relation? Is the distinction important here?
          </li>
          <li>
            Identify one place in Bauldry &#xa7;2.1 where a set-theoretic
            operation (union, intersection, complement, or set difference)
            appears in a definition or theorem. Write out the statement
            using Module 3 notation and explain what the operation is
            doing in context.
          </li>
          <li>
            In 2<ndash/>3 sentences: what is one concept or proof technique
            from Bauldry &#xa7;2.1 that you expect will be important in
            MAT 5610, based on what you have seen so far in this course?
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       LOOKING AHEAD
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod3-br-ahead">
    <title>Looking Ahead</title>
    <p>
      The topology developed in Bauldry &#xa7;2.1 is the setting for the
      definitions of limit and continuity in Bauldry &#xa7;2.2. When Bauldry
      writes <q><m>f</m> is continuous at <m>c</m></q>, the definition
      quantifies over neighborhoods: for every <m>\varepsilon</m>-neighborhood
      of <m>f(c)</m>, there is a <m>\delta</m>-neighborhood of <m>c</m>
      that maps into it. This is a statement about preimages of sets
      under a function, which is exactly the preimage language from Hirst &#xa7;5.4
      that you read in the Study Guide.
    </p>
    <p>
      You will return to these ideas in <xref ref="ch-mod6"/> (Limits, Continuity,
      and Uniform Continuity). For now, the goal is recognition: the
      set-theoretic vocabulary you built in Module 3 is not abstract
      machinery; it is the language of the definitions you will be
      working with throughout MAT 5610.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

</section>
